WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.188
A latent variable is never observed, but
helps explain what is.

00:00:04.188 --> 00:00:06.738
A Gaussian mixture is the simplest case:

00:00:06.738 --> 00:00:09.913
first choose a component, then draw a
value from it.

00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:14.188
Given a point, Bayes says how likely each
component is to have produced it.

00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:15.900
That's its responsibility.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.413
At x equals one, it almost certainly came
from the first.

00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:23.150
Halfway between them, at x equals two,
it's fifty-fifty.

00:00:23.037 --> 00:00:27.249
The log sits outside a sum, so maximising
directly is awkward.

00:00:27.249 --> 00:00:30.162
EM alternates two easy steps instead.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:33.787
The E step computes responsibilities with
the current parameters.

00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:38.762
The M step refits each component,
weighting each point by its
responsibility.

00:00:38.762 --> 00:00:43.587
With these four points and weights, the
first component gets mean zero point
seven.

00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:47.162
Force the weights to zero or one, and this
is k-means.

00:00:47.123 --> 00:00:48.123
Why does this work?

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:50.611
Because the logarithm curves downwards.

00:00:50.611 --> 00:00:53.298
Y is one or four, with equal odds.

00:00:53.298 --> 00:00:54.348
Which is larger:

00:00:54.348 --> 00:00:57.111
the log of the average, or the average of
the log?

00:00:57.111 --> 00:00:58.698
Pause and decide.

00:01:00.161 --> 00:01:02.261
The log of the average is larger:

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:05.698
zero point nine two against zero point six
nine.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:07.773
That's Jensen's inequality.

00:01:07.773 --> 00:01:13.248
Apply it to any distribution over z, and
you get the evidence lower bound, the
ELBO.

00:01:13.248 --> 00:01:16.348
The gap is exactly a KL divergence.

00:01:16.348 --> 00:01:19.332
Split it, and it reads as a reconstruction
score,

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:22.012
minus a penalty for straying from the
prior.

00:01:21.925 --> 00:01:26.251
A variational autoencoder trains an
encoder to output a mean and a width,

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.326
and a decoder to rebuild the input.

00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:30.800
But a random sample blocks the gradient.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.900
So move the randomness into an input:

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.175
z is the mean, plus the width times a
fixed noise.

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:38.712
Now backpropagation passes straight
through.

00:01:38.712 --> 00:01:43.500
Here, z is zero point eight, and the
gradient reaches both mu and sigma.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.600
And the penalty has a closed form:

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.000
zero when the code matches the prior.
